\description{
\code{pswmap()} analyses a randomized trial whose control arm is augmented with one or more external control sources.
It fits source-specific propensity scores, summarizes each source by its odds-weighted mean, builds a robust
meta-analytic predictive (MAP) prior for the trial's control mean, and returns the posterior of the treatment
effect. Covariates may be missing in the external data, but not in the trial. With \code{borrow = FALSE} the trial
is analysed on its own, which gives the reference analysis without borrowing.

This is the function most users need. Advanced users who want to compute and inspect each step themselves can
run the steps one at a time with \code{\link[=psw_weights]{psw_weights()}}, \code{\link[=psw_summaries]{psw_summaries()}}, \code{\link[=psw_reference_scale]{psw_reference_scale()}}, \code{\link[=psw_map_prior]{psw_map_prior()}}
and \code{\link[=psw_posterior]{psw_posterior()}}. With the same settings and seed, this gives exactly the same result.
}
\details{
\strong{The analysis.} For each external source \eqn{H_k}, the propensity score \eqn{e_k(x) = P(Z = 1 \mid X = x)}{e_k(x) = P(Z = 1 | X = x)} is
fitted to the trial (\eqn{Z = 1}) stacked with \eqn{H_k} (\eqn{Z = 0}). Each external subject gets the odds
weight \eqn{w_i = e_k(x_i)/\{1 - e_k(x_i)\}}{w_i = e_k(x_i)/(1 - e_k(x_i))}, with the score capped at \code{ps_cap}. Source \eqn{H_k} is summarized
by its weighted mean \eqn{\bar y_k^w}{ybar_k} and design-based variance \eqn{V_k} (see \code{\link[=psw_summaries]{psw_summaries()}}). The PSW-MAP
prior is the predictive distribution of the control mean \eqn{\theta_0}{theta_0} in the hierarchical model
\deqn{\bar y_k^w \mid \theta_k \sim N(\theta_k, V_k), \qquad \theta_0, \theta_1, \dots, \theta_K \mid \mu, \tau
  \sim N(\mu, \tau^2),}{ybar_k | theta_k ~ N(theta_k, V_k),   theta_0, theta_1, ..., theta_K | mu, tau ~ N(mu, tau^2),}
with \eqn{\tau \sim}{tau ~} half-N\eqn{(0, (\sigma_{ref}/c)^2)}{(0, (sigma_ref/c)^2)}. It is mixed with a weakly informative component of
weight \code{robust_weight} (see \code{\link[=psw_map_prior]{psw_map_prior()}}), updated with the trial's control arm, and combined with the
treated arm's posterior to give the posterior of \eqn{\beta_1 = \theta_T - \theta_0}{beta_1 = theta_T - theta_0} (see \code{\link[=psw_posterior]{psw_posterior()}}).

\strong{Assumptions.} The analysis relies on three assumptions, each addressed by one part of the method:
\itemize{
\item \emph{Measured covariates explain the differences.} Given the covariates, the control outcome of an external
subject has the same distribution as in the trial. The propensity score weights adjust for the measured
differences. Remaining differences between sources (for example in standard of care) are absorbed by the
between-source standard deviation \eqn{\tau}{tau} and, under prior-data conflict, by the robust component.
\item \emph{Overlap.} Every source contains subjects resembling the trial subjects. With poor overlap the weights are
very unequal, the source's variance \eqn{V_k} is large, and the source is discounted. The Kish effective
sample size in \code{sources} shows the overlap of each source.
\item \emph{A missing-data assumption that matches the chosen strategy} (see below).
}

\strong{Missing external covariates} (\code{missing}). Covariates may be missing completely at random (MCAR), at random
given the observed data (MAR), or not at random (MNAR). \code{\link[=psw_weights]{psw_weights()}} gives the definitions and details.
\itemize{
\item \code{"mi"} (recommended) imputes each source on its own, with the outcome in the imputation model. It analyses
every imputed dataset up to the source summaries and combines these by Rubin's rules. Valid under MAR,
including when missingness depends on the outcome.
\item \code{"cc"} gives incomplete external subjects weight 0. Valid when missingness is independent of the outcome given
the covariates, at the cost of less borrowing.
\item \code{"native"} lets the learner handle the missing values itself. Because the trial is complete, a missing value
identifies a subject as external, so these methods move toward complete-case weighting as the missing rate or
the sample size grows, and share its assumptions.
}

Use \code{"mi"} unless you have reason to believe that missingness does not depend on the outcome. Then \code{"cc"} is a
simple alternative that needs no imputation model. The relative weight of incomplete subjects in \code{sources}
shows how much weight incomplete subjects keep under each strategy.

\strong{Choosing \code{tau_c}.} The heterogeneity constant \eqn{c} sets the scale \eqn{\sigma_{ref}/c}{sigma_ref/c} of the half-normal
prior on the between-source standard deviation \eqn{\tau}{tau}. Values 32, 16, 8 and 4 correspond to small,
moderate, substantial and large anticipated heterogeneity (Zhu et al., 2024, following Neuenschwander and
Schmidli, 2020). A smaller \eqn{c} borrows less and protects better against prior-data conflict. There is no
default, because the choice should reflect what is known about the sources.

\strong{Reading the output.} The prior effective sample size (ESS) states how many trial control subjects the external
data are worth. Compare the posterior with that of \code{borrow = FALSE}, the trial alone: the gain from borrowing
shows as a narrower credible interval. A much smaller ESS than the external sample sizes signals poor overlap or heterogeneity
between the sources.

\strong{Defaults.} All other settings default to the values recommended by Guo and Zhu:
\itemize{
\item a main-effects logistic propensity score, capped at 0.95;
\item multiple imputation with 5 imputed datasets;
\item a robust component of weight 0.2 with the information of one subject;
\item the MCMC and mixture settings of \code{\link[=map_control]{map_control()}}.
}

\strong{Reproducibility.} Set \code{seed} to make an analysis reproducible. The seed then governs every random step:
\itemize{
\item the imputations;
\item the random forest and the XGBoost cross-fitting folds;
\item the MCMC sampler and the posterior draws;
\item BART, through bartMachine's own random number generator.
}

Your session's random number stream is restored afterwards. With the default \code{seed = NULL} the analysis continues
the current stream, so calling \code{\link[=set.seed]{set.seed()}} beforehand works as well, except for BART. bartMachine is
reproducible only on one core, its default.
}
